{#
    The rail: the pages of the section the reader is in, not every page there
    is.

    The bar picks the section — Guidelines, Frontend, the design system — and
    this is what is inside the one that was picked. Rendering the whole tree
    put the guidelines under a page about the frontend, which is a sitemap
    rather than navigation.

    **The rail is given its list, not built out of one.** `sds-rail` takes
    entries as data — that is its documented form, and a group is
    `{ label, items }` — so what this file produces is the list and nothing
    else. It used to write `.sds-rail__item` and `.sds-rail__group` and the
    `<details>` around them, which is the element's own markup spelt in a
    second place.

    And the list is worked out in `Navigation\Rail` rather than here: which
    section a page belongs to is the tree walked upwards, which a template can
    only fake. This one faked it by looking for the link that resolves to `#`
    two levels down, so every page deeper than that found no section and got
    the sections back instead — see `ARCHITECTURE.md`.

    `active` is written even when it is -1. It counts over the flattened list,
    groups included, and left off the element falls back to its first item —
    a page the reader is not on, marked as the one they are.
#}
{%- set list = rail(node) -%}
<sds-rail
    label="{{ list.label }}"
    items="{{ list.items|json_encode }}"
    active="{{ list.active }}"
></sds-rail>
